    id: "4096fbd233f7fc0a9ac0",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "Coastal areas in England at risk of erosion to receive £11m in new funding",
    summary: "Exclusive: Laser-equipped drone surveys and road relocations among schemes to get government support Laser-equipped drone surveys, sand dune restoration and moving roads inland are among the projects that will receive government funding to help seaside communities in England adapt to rising seas and coastal erosion accelerated by climate breakdown . More than £11m will be shared between 12 council-led projects that will test ways to spot coastal change earlier and help communities plan for the effects. Continue reading...",
    preview: "Exclusive: Laser-equipped drone surveys and road relocations among schemes to get government support Laser-equipped drone surveys, sand dune restoration and moving roads inland are among the projects that will receive government funding to help seaside communities in England adapt to rising seas and coastal erosion accelerated by climate breakdown . More than £11m will be shared between 12 council-led projects that will test ways to spot coastal change earlier and help communities plan for the effects. Continue reading...",
    body: "Laser-equipped drone surveys, sand dune restoration and moving roads inland are among the projects that will receive government funding to help seaside communities in England adapt to rising seas and coastal erosion accelerated by climate breakdown.\n\nMore than £11m will be shared between 12 council-led projects that will test ways to spot coastal change earlier and help communities plan for the effects.\n\nCoastal erosion is the gradual wearing away and retreat of the sea shoreline, which can destabilise cliffs and eventually force homes and infrastructure to be demolished before they collapse, leaving people homeless. While it is a natural process, scientists say the climate crisis is accelerating the retreat of the UK’s shores.\n\nEmma Hardy MP, the minister for water and flooding, said the funding will help communities manage the “heartbreak” of losing homes to erosion.\n\n“Coastal erosion is going to happen and it’s going to happen at a faster rate, because climate change is real and we’re seeing that impact,” she said. “This is about being prepared for that coastal erosion and having those conversations [with affected residents] early.”\n\nThe Guardian revealed last month that seaside communities along the east coast of England had lost at least 183 homes to coastal erosion and landslides over the past 16 years.\n\nThe Environment Agency (EA) estimates that 3,500 properties in England will be at risk from coastal erosion by 2055, increasing to more than 10,000 properties by 2105.\n\nMore than 6 miles (10km) of major roads, 107 miles of minor roads and 3.5 miles of railway – many serving small, coastal communities – are also at risk from erosion over the next 80 years, according to the EA.\n\nWith devastated villages in East Riding of Yorkshire, Norfolk and Suffolk already receiving specialised funding, Wednesday’s announcement aims to help at-risk councils and residents understand erosion, identify when homes will become unsafe, and prepare to move.\n\nHardy said better monitoring and earlier planning could help councils support residents before erosion made their homes unsafe, reducing the risk of people being forced to leave at short notice and move to temporary accommodation.\n\nJulie Foley, the EA’s director of flood risk strategy and adaptation, said: “Some of the households – who are literally on the edge of the cliff face – they’re incredibly vulnerable people. They’re often some of the most disadvantaged, most vulnerable people in society. They can be very elderly. They may be disabled.”\n\nShe added: “This is investment in communities and engagement and support.”\n\nNorth Yorkshire council, which includes vulnerable coastlines around Filey, Cayton and Scarborough, will use £970,000 of the fund to launch drone surveys. The drones will use laser pulses to measure distances and generate detailed 3D maps of the coast to spot potential cliff movements earlier.\n\nIn Cornwall, which has about 500 homes currently at risk of erosion, dunes protecting the coastline are being washed away due to sea level rise and are not being replenished fast enough. The local authority will receive £2.6m to work on community-led adaptation plans and replace the dunes so that they can provide a “nature-based buffer” against waves that crash on the shore. Plans will be developed with residents in places such as Crackington Haven, Hayle, Perranporth and Portreath.\n\nIn Watchet, Somerset, cliff movement has forced the closure of the B3191 road, with about 20 properties at direct or future risk. A £290,000 project will use 3D modelling and public engagement to help residents understand the risks and shape a long-term response.\n\nIn Redcar and Cleveland, funding will be used to adapt highways, moving a key road into the village of Staithes inland before it collapses due to erosion.\n\nOther local authorities to receive funding include Dorset, Isle of Wight, Northumberland and South Tyneside council. District councils involved include New Forest, South Hams, Swale borough and Wyre council.",
    content_status: "full",
    source: "The Guardian",
    url: "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/25/coastal-areas-england-at-risk-erosion-11m-funding-drones-ea",
