    cat: "geopolitica",
    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "Abdul El-Sayed, seen from Egypt: A smear campaign reflecting the regime's anxiety",
    summary: "Since his election as a US senator in Michigan, the Democrat, a son of Egyptian immigrants, has come under fierce attack from most of Egypt's government-aligned media outlets, who accuse him of being close to the Muslim Brotherhood.",
    preview: "Three weeks after his resounding victory in the Senate race in Michigan during the midterm elections on November 3, Abdul El-Sayed, the son of Egyptian immigrants and now a leading figure of the Democratic Party's left wing, remains a hot topic along the Nile. While Egypt's authorities have yet to comment on the man who, come January 2027, will become the first Muslim US senator, the debate surrounding him is intense on social media and in the press.",
    body: "Three weeks after his resounding victory in the Senate race in Michigan during the midterm elections on November 3, Abdul El-Sayed, the son of Egyptian immigrants and now a leading figure of the Democratic Party's left wing, remains a hot topic along the Nile. While Egypt's authorities have yet to comment on the man who, come January 2027, will become the first Muslim US senator, the debate surrounding him is intense on social media and in the press.\n\nMost media outlets, nearly all loyal to the regime, have worked hard to discredit the senator-elect, who, during his campaign, described President Abdel Fattah el-Sissi's government as \"a military dictatorship funded by our tax dollars here.\" He was referring to the $1.3 billion in annual US aid to Washington's Arab ally, the second-largest recipient of US military assistance after Israel. The candidate argued that taxpayer money would be better spent on healthcare, schools and infrastructure than on foreign wars.\n\nHis stance alarmed Egypt's political and media elite, especially as the 42-year-old doctor-turned-politician won over a large segment of Arab public opinion with his quick wit and blunt criticisms of what he called the \"genocide\" in Gaza. Even Alaa Mubarak, son of former president Hosni Mubarak (1981-2011), posted a video of El-Sayed's victory speech, in which he addressed US Vice President JD Vance, one of his fiercest critics, with a friendly \"habibi,\" an Arabic term of endearment.\n\nYou have 77.65% of this article left to read. The rest is for subscribers only.",
