    id: "d8d731fa8dfe68ae191d",
    cat: "conflitti",
    categories: ["conflitti"],
    title: "'Unlawful de facto exemption': US sitting on 12-year backlog of alleged Israeli rights violations",
    summary: "'Unlawful de facto exemption': US sitting on 12-year backlog of alleged Israeli rights violations Submitted by MEE staff on Tue, 11/24/2026 - 18:17 A report from the Office of The Inspector General has raised concerns that the US has given Israel an 'exemption' from the Leahy Law US Secretary of State Marco Rubio pictured last month (Oliver Contreras/AFP) Off The US Department of State has a 12-year backlog of alleged cases of gross human rights violations in Israel to adjudicate, because the agency has made little effort to trace the end point of US military assistance there, a report quietly posted online by the Office of the Inspector General (OIG) last month showed. The findings \"demonstrate that the Department has created an unlawful de facto exemption to the law for Israel\", Charles Blaha, a state department official who retired in 2023, wrote in a piece for Just Security on Monday. The OIG report hones in on the application of the Leahy Law, which prohibits US assistance to any foreign security forces unit if the secretary of state has credible information that such a unit has committed \"gross violations of human rights\". In a September 2025 report to Congress, the state department said the only units it had assessed to be ineligible for US security assistance were in Ukraine, Egypt and Jordan - even though Israel had previously been cited as having non-traceable assistance (NTA). The NTA means the state department \"cannot identify recipient units in advance of the transfer of assistance or training, or the units are not known with sufficient detail to make Leahy vetting possible\", the OIG report said. It concluded that the state department \"had not developed or distributed comprehensive guidance and training to help staff implement NTA vetting requirements\", and that nearly half of all missions overseas \"did not respond to annual requirements to certify if a country was receiving NTA\". \"For those countries receiving NTA, additional certifications are necessary to validate that foreign security forces are complying with US requirements,\" the report added. In April 2024, under the Biden administration, the state department determined that five Israeli security units had committed grave human rights violations. But it then said that four of them had remedied the violations, and could still receive US security assistance, while the fifth unit would \"remain indefinitely eligible while the State Department engaged with the government of Israel 'on identifying a path to effective remediation of this unit'\". But the state department \"has never delivered a list of ineligible Israeli units to the government of Israel. Not once\", Blaha wrote in June of that year. War on Gaza Blaha, who was the director of two different offices in the Bureau of Democracy, Human Rights, and Labor in the state department from 2013 to 2023, said on Monday that his former employer \"never officially explained its failure to enforce Leahy with regard to Israel, except to say that investigations are ongoing or cases are being reviewed\". \"Behind the scenes, opponents of applying the law to Israel at the State Department and on the National Security Council have advanced specious, legally irrelevant policy arguments,\" he wrote. Israel likely to lose Gaza genocide case if ICJ follows Myanmar ruling, Tel Aviv scholar says ny Blinken reportedly refused to implement Leahy with regard to Israel due to speculation that it would jeopardize unrelated ceasefire negotiations. The argument was far-fetched in 2024, and is obsolete now that Israel has signed the October 2025 Gaza peace plan.\" The US has always had tools at its disposal to pressure allies to change direction, and especially ones who are dependent on US military assistance. These tools include the Leahy Law , which prohibits US military assistance to units that violate human rights laws; the Export Control Act , which bars the transfer of weapons abroad if they could end up threatening US national security; and section 620I of the Foreign Assistance Act , which stipulates that the US government cannot provide weapons to a government that is hindering access to humanitarian aid. The Export Control Act, in particular, states that countries can only receive US weapons if they use them defensively, not offensively. Former state department official Hala Rharrit previously told Middle East Eye that its precisely why Blinken deferred to the refrain that “Israel has a right to defend itself”. “It's not by accident [that] the words that [came] out of his mouth,” she said. Loopholes In December 2024, a month before the Biden administration left power, five Palestinians living in the US and Gaza filed a lawsuit against Blinken over accusations that Washington was not abiding by the Leahy Law and therefore allowing Israel to carry out its unprecedented assault on Gaza. That assault has since been assessed to be a genocide by both the United Nations and Holocaust scholars. More than 74,000 Palestinians have been killed in Gaza since 7 October 2023, with at least 10,000 more missing under the rubble, the Lancet medical journal previously estimated . The lawsuit, which was filed with the help of the rights group Democracy for the Arab World Now (Dawn), outlined how the Biden administration created loopholes that allowed Israel to continue committing human rights violations without a suspension of aid from Washington. One such loophole was the state department's establishment of the \"Israel Leahy Vetting Forum\", which the lawsuit said was designed \"to impede any prompt and effective determination that Israeli units have committed\" gross violations of human rights. Dawn was founded by the late MEE columnist Jamal Khashoggi. One of the plaintiffs in the lawsuit is Gaza-born Ahmed Moor, who is running for the US Senate in 2028. Blaha, who is now a senior adviser to Dawn, said in 2024 that \"the standard for ineligibility is almost impossibly high\" when it comes to Israel. \"A determination that any Israeli unit is Leahy-ineligible rests with the deputy secretary of state. This is true for no other country in the world,\" he said. \"Such determinations are routinely made at the working level, by experts versed in the Leahy Law and knowledgeable about the foreign security units in question.\" The underlying culprit, he added, \"is lack of political will\". US-Israel relations News Post Date Override 0 Update Date Mon, 05/04/2020 - 21:19 Update Date Override 0",
    preview: "'Unlawful de facto exemption': US sitting on 12-year backlog of alleged Israeli rights violations Submitted by MEE staff on Tue, 11/24/2026 - 18:17 A report from the Office of The Inspector General has raised concerns that the US has given Israel an 'exemption' from the Leahy Law US Secretary of State Marco Rubio pictured last month (Oliver Contreras/AFP) Off The US Department of State has a 12-year backlog of alleged cases of gross human rights violations in Israel to adjudicate, because the agency has made little effort to trace the end point of US…",
